%[text] # Overview of Machine Learning for Regression
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text] In this script, we explore why a single straight line fails to capture many real‑world regression problems and use machine learning models to represent curved, oscillatory, and nonstationary behavior. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Regression learner app](text:image:2292)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] Here, we show when linear regression fails and how machine‑learning models better capture complex data and generalize to new conditions.
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternatively, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![Live script code hidden icon](text:image:41b1) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:38a1) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8F8A032D] ![Warning symbol](text:image:99e6) For the best experience, complete each section before moving to the next one. Some sections depend on variables created earlier, so running sections out of order can generate errors.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:9569) and ![Exercise icon](text:image:3571) icons mark two types of interactive activities in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:4f21) icon usually marks an exploration of a concept introduced in the lesson. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:320c) icon marks activities that check your understanding and may be used for grading or completion checks by your instructor.
%%
%[text] ## Straight-Line Regression: A Good Start
%[text] Simple linear regression (SLR) starts with a straight‑line model,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y =  \\beta\_1x + \\beta\_0${"altText":"y =  \beta\_1x + \beta\_0"},
%[text] where $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"}, the slope, and the y-intercept, $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} describe how the response changes with the input. This model is a strong baseline for clean, roughly linear data: it’s easy to compute, easy to interpret, and often reveals the main trend with a single line.
%[text] This overview script introduces why regression problems often require more than a straight line, and how machine‑learning approaches help us model complexity that cannot be linearized or captured by simple transformations.
%[text] SLR works best when:
%[text] - The relationship between variables is roughly linear.
%[text] - The dataset is relatively clean.
%[text] - There are a few features or interactions. \
%[text] When these conditions hold, a line is interpretable, easy to compute, and forms a sensible starting point for exploratory modeling.
%[text]  ![Try this icon](text:image:2912) **Try**. Click **Show Simple Linear Regression** to explore how linear regression behaves on a clean dataset.
  %[control:button:1d50]{"position":[1,2]}
rng shuffle
xLin = linspace(0,10,40)'; % Single feature
yLin = 2.5*xLin + 10 + 2*randn(size(xLin)); % Linear trend + noise

figure %[output:105c48bb]
scatter(xLin,yLin,40,"filled",MarkerFaceAlpha=0.7,SeriesIndex=3) %[output:105c48bb]
grid on %[output:105c48bb]
box on %[output:105c48bb]
hold on %[output:105c48bb]
p = polyfit(xLin,yLin,1);
hAnim = animatedline(LineWidth=2); %[output:105c48bb]
title("Simple Linear Regression Works Well Here",Interpreter="latex") %[output:105c48bb]
xlabel("x",Interpreter="latex") %[output:105c48bb]
ylabel("y",Interpreter="latex") %[output:105c48bb]
axis padded %[output:105c48bb]

% Animate the line drawing from left to right using animatedline
n = numel(xLin);
yVals = polyval(p,xLin);

for k = 1:n
    addpoints(hAnim,xLin(k),yVals(k)); %[output:105c48bb]
    drawnow limitrate
    pause(0.05) % controls animation speed
end

hold off %[output:105c48bb]
%%
%[text] ### **Simple Transformations for Curved Data**
%[text] Real‑world data often follow patterns that a single straight line cannot capture, such as gentle curves or smooth nonlinear trends. In some of these cases, the relationship can be **re‑expressed** so that it becomes approximately linear. For example, power‑law, reciprocal, and exponential relationships can sometimes be transformed into a straight‑line form, allowing us to continue using standard linear regression tools. This approach works only when a **single, monotonic transformation** of the response or predictor can straighten the relationship without distorting the underlying structure of the data. The table below shows common examples of these simple linearizing transformations.
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Fit Type** | **Format** | **Linearized Format** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Exponential | $y=ae^{bx}${"altText":"y=ae^{bx}"}  | ${\\ln(y)}=bx+{\\ln(a)}${"altText":"{\ln(y)}=bx+{\ln(a)}"} |
%[text] | Power | $y=ax^{b}${"altText":"y=ax^{b}"} | ${\\log(y)}=b\\thinspace{\\log(x)}+{\\log(a)}${"altText":"{\log(y)}=b\thinspace{\log(x)}+{\log(a)}"} |
%[text] | Saturation | $y=\\frac{x}{ax+b}${"altText":"y=\frac{x}{ax+b}"} | ${\\frac{1}y}=b\\frac{1}x+{a}${"altText":"{\frac{1}y}=b\frac{1}x+{a}"} |
%[text] | S-curve\* | $y=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-bx}}${"altText":"y=\frac{1}{1+e^{-bx}}"} | $\\ln\\left(\\frac{y}{1-y}\\right) =bx${"altText":"\ln\left(\frac{y}{1-y}\right) =bx"} |
%[text:table]
%[text] \*The S-curve linearization applies only when the response is bounded between 0 and 1.
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_2207] ![MATLAB Icon](text:image:1448) **Pro-tip**. Review these simple transformations in [Regression Basics](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/93435-regression-basics).
%%
%[text] ### **Diagnosing When a Line is No Longer Enough**
%[text] A straight line is a useful starting point, but it is not always enough. We diagnose this by comparing the fitted line with the data and then checking the residuals. If the residuals show curvature, repeating waves, or a clear change in spread, the model is missing structure.
%[text] **Oscillatory patterns**, for example, where the response repeatedly rises and falls, cannot be captured by a single slope across the full input range.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y=\\sin(2\\pi(1+3x)x)${"altText":"y=\sin(2\pi(1+3x)x)"},
%[text] where the oscillation rate varies with $x${"altText":"x"}, making the relationship difficult to linearize with a single transformation.
%[text] Heteroscedastic data, for example, where the amount of scatter varies across the input range, may follow a general trend but yield predictions with varying uncertainty across different regions.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = \\sin(2\\pi x)+\\sigma(x)\\epsilon${"altText":"y = \sin(2\pi x)+\sigma(x)\epsilon"}, $\\sigma(x)=0.2+0.8x${"altText":"\sigma(x)=0.2+0.8x"}.
%[text] The main idea is not the formula; it is that the residual plot fans out or narrows as $x${"altText":"x"} changes, so the spread is not roughly constant.
%[text] When these patterns remain after simple transformations, the issue is not just the axis scale. The model either needs better features or a more flexible learning method that bridges straight-line regression to machine learning.
%[text] **Use residuals as a decision tool.**
%[text] - fluctuate randomly around zero
%[text] - show no obvious curvature
%[text] - maintain roughly constant spread
%[text] - avoid repeating patterns. \
%[text]  ![Try this icon](text:image:1f3b) **Try**. Then choose a function from **Select a function**, choose an axis from **Select an axis**, and click **Plot Data** to see which transformations best linearize the function and produce well-behaved residuals.
%[text] ![MATLAB Icon](text:image:23a0) **Pro-tip**. Review Assumptions of linearity and curvilinear regression in [Regression Basics](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/93435-regression-basics).
SpecialFunc = "Oscillatory"; %[control:dropdown:2167]{"position":[15,28]}
switch SpecialFunc
    case "Select"
        warning("Select a valid option.")
        return
    case "Oscillatory"
        [x,y] = OscillData;
    case "Heteroscedastic"
        [x,y] = HeteroData;
    case "Unknown"
        [x,y] = Unknown;
end

AX = "semi-logy"; % Use this dropdown menu to select an axis on which to plot the data %[control:dropdown:3a3f]{"position":[6,17]}
plotDataByAxis(AX,x,y,SpecialFunc) %[output:625858f3]
  % Click this button to plot the data %[control:button:1acd]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text]  ![Exercise icon](text:image:97d8) **Exercise 1.** Which dataset(s) are inappropriate for a straight-line model?
if ~strcmp(SpecialFunc,"Select") %[output:group:95aef092]
    checkAnswer("Q1",[true,true,false]) %[control:checkbox:8c82]{"position":[23,27]} %[control:checkbox:9a4a]{"position":[28,32]} %[control:checkbox:38d0]{"position":[33,38]} %[output:5c3ccc7a]
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
    return
end %[output:group:95aef092]
  %[control:button:3d1e]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ## When Models Are Too Simple or Too Complex
%[text] Linear models can only take us so far, leading us toward more flexible approaches. Before moving to machine learning, we must avoid a new set of problems: models that are either too simple or too complex. As the following examples will show, models can fail in two ways—by being too rigid or by fitting noise. This introduces a key idea: **underfitting** occurs when a model is too simple to capture the true relationship, while **overfitting** occurs when a model is so complex that it memorizes random variation. Underfitting is associated with high bias (the model is too rigid), while overfitting is associated with high variance (the model becomes too sensitive to noise). A perfect fit to the training data does not guarantee good performance. The goal is to find a balance by learning patterns that generalize to new data.
%[text]  ![Try this icon](text:image:01af) **Try**. Click **Compare Fit Models** to visualize the differences between underfitting, overfitting, and good fitting.
  %[control:button:6a8a]{"position":[1,2]}
% Underfit vs Good Fit vs Overfit
rng shuffle
xDemo = linspace(0,1,25)';
yTrue = @(x) sin(2*pi*x);
yDemo = yTrue(xDemo) + 0.25*randn(size(xDemo));

xFine = linspace(0,1,400)';
yTrueFine = yTrue(xFine);

% Underfit: degree 1 polynomial
pUnder = polyfit(xDemo,yDemo,1);
yUnder = polyval(pUnder,xFine);

% Good-fit: degree 5
pGood = polyfit(xDemo,yDemo,5);
yGood = polyval(pGood,xFine);

% Overfit: degree 14
pOver = polyfit(xDemo,yDemo,14);
yOver = polyval(pOver,xFine);

figure %[output:2432b831]
g = gcf;
g.Units = "normalized";
g.OuterPosition = [0 0 0.5 0.5]; %[output:2432b831]
tiledlayout(1,3); %[output:2432b831]

nexttile %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yTrueFine,"--",LineWidth=1.2,SeriesIndex=6) %[output:2432b831]
hold on %[output:2432b831]
scatter(xDemo,yDemo,30,"filled",MarkerFaceAlpha=0.6,SeriesIndex=3) %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yUnder,"-",LineWidth=1.6,SeriesIndex="none") %[output:2432b831]
title("Underfit (Too Simple)",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
xlabel("x",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
ylabel("y",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
legend("True pattern","Data","Degree 1",Location="southoutside") %[output:2432b831]

nexttile %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yTrueFine,"--",LineWidth=1.2,SeriesIndex=6) %[output:2432b831]
hold on %[output:2432b831]
scatter(xDemo,yDemo,30,"filled",MarkerFaceAlpha=0.6,SeriesIndex=3) %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yGood,"-",LineWidth=1.6,SeriesIndex="none") %[output:2432b831]
title("Good Fit (Just Right)",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
xlabel("x",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
ylabel("y",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
legend("True pattern","Data","Degree 5",Location="southoutside") %[output:2432b831]

nexttile %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yTrueFine,"--",LineWidth=1.2,SeriesIndex=6) %[output:2432b831]
hold on %[output:2432b831]
scatter(xDemo,yDemo,30,"filled",MarkerFaceAlpha=0.6,SeriesIndex=3) %[output:2432b831]
plot(xFine,yOver,"-",LineWidth=1.6,SeriesIndex="none") %[output:2432b831]
title("Overfit (Too Complex)",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
xlabel("x",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
ylabel("y",Interpreter="latex") %[output:2432b831]
legend("True pattern","Data","Degree 14",Location="southoutside") %[output:2432b831]
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:7773) **Exercise 2.** Which curve is likely to perform best on new data?
checkAnswer("Q2","Degree 5"); %[control:dropdown:755a]{"position":[18,28]} %[output:8f1b4dd9]
  %[control:button:0844]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Why Use Machine Learning for Regression 
%[text] If simple models underfit and complex models risk overfitting, how do we build better predictors? Machine learning offers flexible methods that can learn patterns a straight line cannot represent.
%[text] Machine learning (ML) for regression is especially valuable when real-world behavior is complex, nonlinear, or influenced by several interacting inputs. In this lesson sequence, the main flexible model families you will meet next are **decision trees** and **ensemble methods**, which are useful when the relationship between inputs and outputs is one or more of the following:
%[text] - Nonlinear
%[text] - Multi‑factor and interactive
%[text] - Hard or impossible to linearize
%[text] - Nonstationary (changing shape or variance across operating conditions) \
%[text] **Real-world examples include:**
%[text] - **Electric vehicles**: ML combines speed, temperature, road grade (how steep the road is), and accessory loads to predict energy use more accurately than a single equation.
%[text] - **Energy forecasting**: ML captures seasonal and weather‑driven patterns that shift hour‑to‑hour and day‑to‑day.
%[text] - **Wind‑tunnel testing**: ML handles aerodynamic regimes where simple formulas break down.
%[text] - **Batteries**: ML learns degradation patterns from voltage, current, and temperature histories. \
%[text] These same types of problems are addressed in practical MATLAB‑based applications, such as:
%[text] - [Improving Vehicle Predictive Maintenance Efficiency](https://www.mathworks.com/company/user_stories/improving-vehicle-predictive-maintenance-efficiency.html)
%[text] - [Machine Learning Advances Wind Turbine Performance](https://www.mathworks.com/company/user_stories/machine-learning-advances-wind-turbine-performance.html)
%[text] - [VGEN Develops Virtual Power Plant with Deep Learning and Machine Learning](https://www.mathworks.com/company/user_stories/vgen-develops-virtual-power-plant-with-deep-learning-and-machine-learning.html)
%[text] - [Energy Monitoring with Machine Learning](https://www.mathworks.com/company/user_stories/cadmus-collects-and-analyzes-energy-data-in-near-real-time-using-matlab-and-the-thingSpeak-internet-of-things-platform.html) \
%[text] Together, these examples highlight how ML excels at learning unknown shapes, handling interacting features, and delivering predictions that generalize beyond the data used to train them.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Electric vehicle charging example that connects the lesson to predicting EV energy use from speed, temperature, road grade, and accessory loads](text:image:610e)
%[text] Once we've determined that a straight-line model is inadequate, there are two broad ways to improve it. We can engineer better features while still using a linear model, or we can adopt a more flexible machine-learning model. The next activity explores the first approach.
%%
%[text] ### Feature Engineering: Improving the Baseline Model 
%[text] Residual diagnostics showed that a straight-line model can miss important structure. Before replacing the model entirely, one common strategy is to improve the representation of the data through feature engineering. By creating more informative input features, a simple linear model can often capture relationships that were previously hidden. When the line fails, the next question is what to change. Sometimes we add or select better features, and sometimes we switch to a more flexible model such as a tree-based method. Here we focus on diagnosis by showing how added features reduce residual structure. Compare residual plots before and after adding features to assess improvements in fit, then use the [ANOVA](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/anova.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_anova) table to quantify those gains, as stronger models explain more variation and leave smaller residual errors.
%[text] ![MATLAB Icon](text:image:9cbb) **Pro-tip**. Review ANOVA in [Regression Basics](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/93435-regression-basics).
%[text]  ![Try this icon](text:image:6ed9) **Try**. Then choose a dataset from **Select an option** and click **Fit a Line** to compare how the residual plots change.
rng(42)
x = linspace(0,2*pi,100)';

% True signal
yTrue = 2 + 0.8*sin(2*x);

shape = "Function B"; %[control:dropdown:1ab9]{"position":[9,21]}
  %[control:button:7cb7]{"position":[1,2]}

switch shape
    case "Function A"
        % Linear relationship with additive Gaussian noise.
        % y ≈ 0.25*x plus random noise (std ~0.2). Single feature X = x.
        y = 0.25*x + 0.2*randn(size(x));
        X = x;

    case "Function B"
        % Noisy sinusoidal true signal: yTrue = 2 + 0.8*sin(2*x).
        % Adds Gaussian noise (std ~0.3). Features contain sin(2x) and cos(2x),
        % which capture the underlying periodic components.
        y = yTrue + 0.3*randn(size(x));
        X = [sin(2*x),cos(2*x)];

    case "Function C"
        % Heteroscedastic noise: noise standard deviation grows with x.
        % yTrue plus noise with sigma = 0.05 + 0.2*(x/max(x)).
        % Features include sin(2x) and an interaction term x.*sin(2x),
        % allowing a model to capture amplitude modulation over x.
        sigma = 0.05 + 0.2*(x/max(x));
        y = yTrue + sigma.*randn(size(x));
        X = [sin(2*x),x.*sin(2*x)];

    case "Function D"
        % True periodic signal yTrue plus two added components:
        % - Gaussian noise (std ~0.3)
        % - Additional sinusoid 0.5*sin(3x) introducing a different frequency.
        % Features include sin(2x), sin(3x), and cos(2x) so models can fit both
        % base and added frequencies and phase information.
        y = yTrue + 0.3*randn(size(x)) + 0.5*sin(3*x);
        X = [sin(2*x),sin(3*x),cos(2*x)];

    otherwise
        warning("Select a valid option.")
        return
end

figure
g = gcf;
g.Units = "normalized";
g.OuterPosition = [0 0 0.5 0.5];
PlotTransClean(x,y,X) %[output:8517638d] %[output:12a5e1b2] %[output:9be1c69f] %[output:61fd4f32] %[output:5d791cc2]
%[text] ![](text:image:3afb) **Reflect**. Do residuals look random or patterned? Would a more flexible model help?
%%
%[text] ### A Choice Between Linear Regression and Machine Learning
%[text] To fairly compare models, we need a way to estimate how well they perform on new, unseen data. We do this by using 5‑fold [cross‑validation](https://www.mathworks.com/discovery/cross-validation.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_cross%20%20validation) (**CV**) to compare a simple linear regression (SLR) model with a more flexible machine learning model. In the 5‑fold **CV**, we split the data into five parts. Each time, we train the model on four parts and test it on the remaining part. We repeat this until every part has been used for testing once.
%[text] For each test set, we compute the **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**, which measures how far off the model’s predictions are from the actual values (on average). The **CV‑MSE** is simply the average of these test errors across all five runs. This gives us a better estimate of how the model will perform on new, unseen data.
%[text] If the tree model has a lower **CV‑MSE** than the straight-line model across the folds, it suggests the relationship in the data is more complex than a straight line can capture (for example, it might include curves or thresholds). At this point, we can either improve the linear model by adding better features or switch to a more flexible model and tune it using cross-validation.
%[text] In this example, the dataset comes from a noisy, nonlinear sinusoidal relationship, where a straight-line model may not fully capture the underlying pattern. The key idea is to choose the model with the lowest **CV‑MSE**: the one that predicts new data best, not the one that just fits the training data most closely. This is a preview of the tree-based model comparisons you will make later in the course.
%[text] The dashed true curve in the plot is shown only for instruction. In practice, model selection should rely on **CV-MSE** and held-out predictions rather than access to the true generating function.
%[text]  ![Try this icon](text:image:0a45) **Try**. Click **Perform Cross-Validation** to compare the models.
  %[control:button:18a4]{"position":[1,2]}
rng shuffle
n = 120;
x = linspace(0,2*pi,n)';
yTrue = 2 + 0.8*sin(2*x) + 0.4*sin(5*x);
y = yTrue + 0.5*randn(n,1);

cv = cvpartition(n,KFold=5);

% --- Model 1: Simple Linear Regression
linMSE = zeros(cv.NumTestSets,1);
for k = 1:cv.NumTestSets
    tr = training(cv,k); te = test(cv,k);
    mdl = fitlm(x(tr),y(tr));
    yhat = predict(mdl,x(te));
    linMSE(k) = mean((y(te) - yhat).^2);
end

% --- Model 2: Regression Tree (non parametric, flexible)
treeMSE = zeros(cv.NumTestSets,1);
for k = 1:cv.NumTestSets
    tr = training(cv,k);
    te = test(cv,k);
    mdl = fitrtree(x(tr),y(tr));
    yhat = predict(mdl,x(te));
    treeMSE(k) = mean((y(te) - yhat).^2);
end

disp("Mean CV-MSE (Simple Linear Regression Model):   " + num2str(mean(linMSE),'%.3f')); %[output:7fd024bf]
disp("Mean CV-MSE (Machine Learning Model (Regression Tree)):  " + num2str(mean(treeMSE),'%.3f')); %[output:42f31d40]

% Fit models
mdlLin  = fitlm(x,y);
mdlTree = fitrtree(x,y);

% Fine grid
xf = linspace(min(x),max(x),400)';
yTrueFine = 2 + 0.8*sin(2*xf) + 0.4*sin(5*xf);
yLin  = predict(mdlLin,xf);
yTree = predict(mdlTree,xf);

figure %[output:015f6768]
g = gcf;
g.Units = "normalized";
g.OuterPosition = [0 0 0.5 0.5]; %[output:015f6768]
scatter(x,y,"filled",SeriesIndex=3,DisplayName="Data") %[output:015f6768]
hold on %[output:015f6768]
box on %[output:015f6768]

hTrue = animatedline(Linestyle="--",LineWidth=1.2,SeriesIndex=4,DisplayName="True trend (shown only for instruction)"); %[output:015f6768]
hLin  = animatedline(Linestyle="-",LineWidth=1.5,SeriesIndex=5,DisplayName="Simple Linear Regression"); %[output:015f6768]
hTree = animatedline(Linestyle="-",LineWidth=1.5,SeriesIndex=6,DisplayName="Machine Learning Model"); %[output:015f6768]

xlabel("x",Interpreter="latex") %[output:015f6768]
ylabel("y",Interpreter="latex") %[output:015f6768]

drawnow
pauseTime = 0.01;

% TRUE function
clearpoints(hTrue)

legend("show",Location="best") %[output:015f6768]

for i = 1:length(xf)
    addpoints(hTrue,xf(i),yTrueFine(i)) %[output:015f6768]
    addpoints(hLin,xf(i),yLin(i))
    addpoints(hTree,xf(i),yTree(i))
    drawnow limitrate
    pause(pauseTime)
end

hold off %[output:015f6768]
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:51ee) **Reflect**. Looking at the mean **CV‑MSE** and the curves on the plot, which model is more likely to generalize and why?
%%
%[text] ### A Common Machine Learning Pipeline
%[text] The previous sections illustrate part of a broader machine learning pipeline. In practice, model development is iterative: we prepare the data, establish a baseline, evaluate performance, and improve the model only when the evidence supports it. For regression, this process typically follows a simple pattern:
%[text] - **Define goal & prepare data** – clarify what to predict; clean data and create useful features
%[text] - **Train baseline model** – build a simple starting point
%[text] - **Evaluate** – check performance with validation or cross-validation
%[text] - **Improve** – refine features, models, or settings as needed
%[text] - **Deploy** – use the final model in practice \
%[text] This common ML pipeline reflects a core principle: start simple, test generalization, and increase complexity only when it leads to measurable improvement.
%[text] Within the workflow introduced earlier, this module primarily covers the **Improve** stage, with some connection to **Train**:
%[text] - **Regularization and feature selection** control model complexity and help prevent overfitting
%[text] - **Decision trees and ensemble methods** introduce more flexible models to capture nonlinear behavior
%[text] - **Gradient descent** explains how many models are trained by iteratively improving their parameters \
%[text] Together, these topics show how to move beyond a baseline model and make targeted improvements based on evidence from evaluation.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3dcf) **Exercise 3.** What is the primary goal of training a regression model?
checkAnswer("Q3","To learn the relationship between inputs and a continuous output"); %[control:dropdown:9923]{"position":[18,84]} %[output:5e0cfc4c]
  %[control:button:1cb7]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] In this overview, we focused on the middle of the workflow: building a baseline model, diagnosing its limitations, improving it through feature engineering, and comparing alternative models with cross-validation. The remaining lessons build on these ideas by introducing regularization, decision trees, ensemble methods, and optimization techniques for training more advanced models.
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] Through this lesson, we used a straight-line model as a baseline, saw how residuals reveal when it fails, and previewed how cross-validation helps compare stronger candidates. That gives us the starting point for the rest of the sequence: diagnose the baseline first, then improve the model in a disciplined way.
%[text] ### Key Takeaways
%[text] - Start by defining the prediction goal clearly: what to predict, which inputs are available, and what counts as a useful prediction.
%[text] - Start with simple linear regression as a baseline when the data are clean and roughly linear.
%[text] - Use residual plots to detect curvature, oscillation, changing variance, or other patterns a straight line cannot capture.
%[text] - Consider feature engineering or machine learning models when transformations and simple linear models are not enough.
%[text] - Balance model flexibility with generalization by avoiding both underfitting and overfitting.
%[text] - After the baseline check, common next steps are feature selection and regularization, tree-based modeling, and optimization-driven training.
%[text] - Use cross-validation and prediction error metrics, such as CV-MSE, to compare models on unseen data. \
%%
%[text] ## Further Exploration
%[text] Review the [Additional Resources](file:../AdditionalResources.m:M_2e47) section to find more materials for further learning.
%%
%[text] [⇦ Return to Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
